 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 60,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "RED TARN. A tarn is a mountain pond left behind by a glacier, and this one sits in a bowl of crag with one way in. Whoever came up the scree: well done. The view is better than the walk."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "SCAFELL PIKE. At 978 metres, the highest point in England. The cairn is a pile of stones with a lot of opinions. A good walker adds a stone, takes a photo, and leaves before the cloud decides to stay."
  },
  {
   "x": 7,
   "y": 50,
   "text": "A HILL FARM. The woman who lived here wrote little books about animals in waistcoats, bred Herdwick sheep, and left four thousand acres to the nation. The sheep are still here, and still unimpressed."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 51,
   "text": "HERDWICK COUNTRY. These grey, woolly sheep have been on the fells since the Vikings. A Herdwick lamb is born black and goes grey, like a good wizard. They know their own patch of hill and teach it to their lambs."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "THE SLATE MINE. Green slate has been cut from this fell for centuries, and the mountain is full of tunnels. The rock is dressed by hand, split with a chisel, and used to roof half the Lakes. The other half is under cloud."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE PASS. One of the oldest packhorse routes in the Lakes, and a very good place for the fog to hold a meeting. If the cloud comes down, stop, wait and have your sandwich."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "THE TARN. A glacier scooped this out and left it to fill with rain, which the Lakes have supplied ever since. The nearby beck is full of small fish, who have never seen a bad day because they live in the wet."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 26,
   "plaque": "lake_district_norse_words",
   "people": "norse",
   "title": "Norse on the Fells",
   "text": "Norse settlers farmed these valleys and left their words all over the map. Fell is Old Norse 'fjall', a mountain; beck is 'bekkr', a stream; tarn is 'tjorn', a pool; and every 'thwaite' was a clearing. Ask for directions here and you are half speaking Norse."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 39,
   "plaque": "lake_district_one_lake",
   "title": "Only One Lake",
   "text": "Strictly speaking, the Lake District has only one lake: Bassenthwaite Lake. The rest are meres, waters and tarns, like Windermere, Ullswater and Wastwater. It is the best pub quiz answer in Cumbria, and Cumbrians will tell you so whether you asked or not."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "arts_wordsworth_daffodils",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Wordsworth's Daffodils",
   "text": "On 15 April 1802 William Wordsworth and his sister Dorothy walked by Ullswater and found a long belt of daffodils tossing in the wind. Dorothy wrote it up in her journal that night; William turned it into 'I wandered lonely as a cloud', published in 1807. He lived at Dove Cottage in Grasmere from 1799, became Poet Laureate in 1843 and died at Rydal Mount in 1850. The daffodils still come up every April, unbothered by fame."
  },
  {
   "x": 4,
   "y": 51,
   "plaque": "arts_beatrix_potter",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Beatrix Potter",
   "text": "Beatrix Potter bought Hill Top farm at Near Sawrey in 1905 with the money from The Tale of Peter Rabbit, published in 1902. She painted the lanes and gardens into her books, bred prize Herdwick sheep, and bought up fell farms as they came on the market. When she died in 1943 she left fifteen farms and more than four thousand acres to the National Trust, with the Herdwicks to stay on them. Mr McGregor's garden is not open to the public."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "lake_district_wrestling",
   "title": "Grasmere Sports",
   "text": "GRASMERE SPORTS. Held every August since 1852, with fell races, hound trails and Cumberland and Westmorland wrestling. Wrestlers start chest to chest, arms locked round each other's backs. You lose by touching the ground with anything but your feet, or by letting go of the hold. The throws have names like the hipe, the buttock and the cross-buttock, and the wrestlers wear embroidered trunks over long johns."
  }
 ],
